<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pactolus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sart Çayı</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pactolus is a small river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> that flows from Mount Tmolus past the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> into the Gediz, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a>. In antiquity it carried grains of electrum, a natural alloy of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, which enriched the Lydian kings and supplied the first coins. Greek myth explained the gold by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">King Midas</a> washing away his golden touch in its waters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pactolus.jpg" alt="Pactolus" width="294" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spiridon Ion Cepleanu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BA%CF%84%CF%89%CE%BB%CF%8C%CF%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
